WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:07.560
In 1976, Carlo Cipolla, a professor of&nbsp;
economic history, derived a social law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:13.560
by which we can group people into four&nbsp;
categories: The first he called “the helpless”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:21.180
the second are “the intelligent”, the third are&nbsp;
“the bandits”, and the last are “the stupid”.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.880
The stupid person is the most&nbsp;
dangerous type of person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:33.420
and in groups far more powerful than the Mafia,&nbsp;
the Military or Communism, Cipolla warned.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.840
To arrive at this conclusion, we need to&nbsp;
understand his 5 Basic Laws of Human Stupidity.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:52.800
1: Always and inevitably, everyone underestimates&nbsp;
the number of stupid individuals in circulation.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:01:01.320
2: The probability that a person is stupid&nbsp;
is independent of any other characteristic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.240
of that person. Education, wealth or&nbsp;
status have nothing to do with it.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:15.840
3: A stupid person is someone who causes losses&nbsp;
to other people while himself deriving no gain.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:25.440
4: Non-stupid people always underestimate&nbsp;
the damaging power of stupid people and keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.600
forgetting that to deal with stupid people&nbsp;
always turns out to be a costly mistake.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.020
5: A stupid person is the most dangerous type&nbsp;
of person, even more dangerous than a bandit.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:49.140
Cipolla then considers four factors of human&nbsp;
behavior. A person can cause benefits to others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.240
benefits to themselves, losses to&nbsp;
others and losses to themselves.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.320
If Tom takes an action and suffers a loss,&nbsp;
he’s helpless. If Tom benefits himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.960
while also benefiting Jerry, he’s&nbsp;
intelligent. If Tom helps himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.800
but causes Jerry a loss, he's a bandit. And if&nbsp;
Tom does something that’s of no benefit to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.920
but causes Jerry a loss he’s stupid.&nbsp;
Ineffectual people are in the center.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.180
Let's now look at the effects&nbsp;
of these groups on society.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.560
Helpless people contribute to society&nbsp;
but are taken advantage of by others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.940
especially bandits. And so&nbsp;
their contribution is limited.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.440
Note that extreme altruists&nbsp;
or pacifists may willingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.800
accept a place in this category for moral reasons.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:50.100
Intelligent people contribute&nbsp;
to society and leverage their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.680
contributions into reciprocal benefits.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.160
Their actions lead to a net gain to society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.540
which is why helpless people should&nbsp;
always support intelligent ones.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.340
Bandits pursue their own self-interest&nbsp;
and enrich themselves, even when doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.280
so poses a harm to society. Helpless and&nbsp;
intelligent people should try to stop them.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.980
Stupid people always contribute to a net loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.600
to society. But not only that. As&nbsp;
they do so for no obvious reason,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.240
Their actions also frustrate,&nbsp;
anger and confuse everyone else.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:38.760
Against major cultural trends among his fellow&nbsp;
intellectuals, Cipolla was convinced that men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.460
are not equal. That some are stupid and others&nbsp;
are not, and that the difference is determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:50.820
by nature and nothing else. One is stupid&nbsp;
in the same way one is red-haired, he wrote.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:58.260
Cipolla warned that the damaging potential&nbsp;
of the stupid person therefore depends on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:03.600
the amount of stupidity inherited, as well&nbsp;
as their position of power in a society.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.600
Among bureaucrats, generals and politicians one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.880
has little difficulty in finding clear&nbsp;
examples of basically stupid individuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.520
whose damaging capacity was alarmingly enhanced&nbsp;
by the position of the power they occupied.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:28.620
What are your thoughts on stupidity? Are&nbsp;
people born or made stupid? Or is it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:33.300
as usual, more complex, and is there&nbsp;
an interrelationship between the two?&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.800
Share your thoughts, and read more&nbsp;
about Cipolla's work and amusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.960
essay — which originally was only intended&nbsp;
for friends — in the descriptions below.

